/-!
# Annihilators of Kähler differentials modulo coordinate differentials

Let `R` be an algebra over a field `k`, and let `xⱼ ∈ R` and `k`-derivations `δᵢ` of `R` be dual,
`δᵢ xⱼ = δᵢⱼ`. Write `N ⊆ Ω[R⁄k]` for the submodule spanned by the coordinate differentials `dxⱼ`
and `I` for the annihilator of `Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ N`. The Lie derivative `L_{δᵢ}` sends `dxⱼ` to
`d(δᵢ xⱼ) = 0`, so it maps `N` into itself (`Derivation.kaehlerLieDeriv_mem_span_range_D`), and
hence `I` is `δᵢ`-stable (`Derivation.apply_mem_annihilator_quotient`).

In characteristic zero, every prime `P` minimal over `I` is then `δᵢ`-stable as well
(`Derivation.apply_mem_of_mem_minimalPrimes`), so the images of the `xⱼ` in `R ⧸ P` are
algebraically independent over `k` (`TauCeti.algebraicIndependent_mk_of_forall_apply_mem`), and
the residue field at `P` has transcendence degree at least the number of coordinates over `k`.
This bound enters the differential smoothness criterion in characteristic zero: for the local
ring `R` of a reduced scheme of pure dimension `d` with a surjection `Ω[R⁄k] → Rᵈ`, it forces such a
`P` to be a minimal prime of `R`, at which `Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ N` vanishes; so `Ω[R⁄k] = N` is free.

Characteristic zero is needed. For the cusp `R = 𝔽₂[x, y] ⧸ (y² - x³)` with the coordinate `y` and
`δ = ∂/∂y` (well defined since `∂(y² - x³)/∂y = 2y = 0`), `Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ R dy` is generated by `dx`,
which is killed by `x²`, and the prime `(x, y)` minimal over the annihilator `(x²)` contains `y`,
whose image in `R ⧸ (x, y) = 𝔽₂` is algebraic.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.KaehlerDifferential.algebraicIndependent_mk_of_mem_minimalPrimes`: in characteristic
  zero, coordinates with dual derivations are algebraically independent modulo every prime minimal
  over the annihilator of the differentials modulo coordinate differentials.

## References

* A. Nobile, *Notes on Nash modification*, arXiv:2404.09102, Section 2, proof of Proposition 2.3.
* E. Hamann, *A question about differential ideals*, arXiv:math/0311006, proof of Lemma 0.1.
-/

public section

open KaehlerDifferential

namespace TauCeti.KaehlerDifferential

/-- **Coordinates stay algebraically independent modulo a prime minimal over the annihilator of
the differentials modulo coordinate differentials**, in characteristic zero. Let `R` be an algebra
over a field `k` of characteristic zero, and let `xⱼ ∈ R` and `k`-derivations `δᵢ` of `R` satisfy
`δᵢ xⱼ = δᵢⱼ`. If `P` is a prime minimal over the annihilator of `Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ span {dxⱼ}`, then the
images of the `xⱼ` in `R ⧸ P` are algebraically independent over `k`. -/
theorem algebraicIndependent_mk_of_mem_minimalPrimes {k R ι : Type*} [Field k] [CharZero k]
    [CommRing R] [Algebra k R] [DecidableEq ι] {x : ι → R} {δ : ι → Derivation k R R}
    (hδ : ∀ i j, δ i (x j) = if i = j then 1 else 0) {P : Ideal R}
    (hP : P ∈ (Module.annihilator R
      (Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ Submodule.span R (Set.range (D k R ∘ x)))).minimalPrimes) :
    AlgebraicIndependent k (Ideal.Quotient.mk P ∘ x) := by
  have hPrime := hP.isPrime
  -- The annihilator is stable under every `δᵢ`, because the Lie derivative along `δᵢ` kills the
  -- coordinate differentials.
  have hI (i : ι) (a : R)
      (ha : a ∈ Module.annihilator R (Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ Submodule.span R (Set.range (D k R ∘ x)))) :
      δ i a ∈ Module.annihilator R (Ω[R⁄k] ⧸ Submodule.span R (Set.range (D k R ∘ x))) :=
    (δ i).apply_mem_annihilator_quotient
      ((δ i).kaehlerLieDeriv_smul (δ i).D_apply_algebraMap_eq_zero)
      (fun _ ↦ (δ i).kaehlerLieDeriv_mem_span_range_D _ fun j ↦ by simp [hδ i j, apply_ite]) ha
  -- No positive integer lies in `P`, since positive integers are units of the `k`-algebra `R`.
  have hchar (n : ℕ) (hn : (n : R) ∈ P) : n = 0 := by
    by_contra hn0
    refine hPrime.ne_top (P.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem hn ?_)
    simpa using ((isUnit_iff_ne_zero.mpr (Nat.cast_ne_zero.mpr hn0 : (n : k) ≠ 0)).map
      (algebraMap k R))
  exact algebraicIndependent_mk_of_forall_apply_mem hδ hPrime.ne_top
    fun i _ ha ↦ (δ i).apply_mem_of_mem_minimalPrimes (hI i) hP hchar ha

end TauCeti.KaehlerDifferential
